{#
  The skyline behind the hero card. One drawing, everywhere, and deliberately nobody in
  particular.

  **Three attempts got here.** Hand-drawn per city read as three interchangeable towns. Computed
  per city from OpenStreetMap building heights was honest, correct, and looked worse than either:
  an upper envelope has ink in every column, one flat weight across the whole width, and no near
  or far - so whatever the opacity, it is a bar rather than a cityscape. That is a property of
  taking an envelope, not a thing that can be tuned out of one.

  What makes this read as a city is the three things an envelope cannot have:

    1. **Sky between the buildings.** The near layer returns to the ground repeatedly, and those
       gaps are where the far ridge shows. A silhouette with no gaps is a footer.
    2. **Two weights.** The far ridge is inked at about a third of the near layer, which is the
       whole of the depth. It is continuous on purpose: it is the mass you see THROUGH the gaps.
    3. **Variety, then restraint.** Pitched roofs, a stepped block, a chimney, two cupolas, a
       sawtooth works and one church spire with air either side of it. A row of rectangles at
       different heights is the version of this that looks generated, and it is what the first
       attempt was.

  **2400 x 100, which is 24:1, and that ratio is load-bearing.** The band is
  `xMidYMax slice`, and slice crops whichever axis has slack - when the band is FLATTER than the
  art, it crops the top, which is where the spire is. That has bitten twice. The widest card this
  layout produces is 1472px against an 80px band, which is 18.4:1, so drawing at
  24:1 leaves margin and the crop is always horizontal. Both layers run from -260 to 2660
  rather than 0 to 2400, so a horizontal crop can never find an end and leave a seam.

  A generic skyline is the one thing that can be cropped anywhere without loss, which is the real
  compensation for giving up the per-city claim: on a phone you see about a quarter of it, and any
  quarter is a town.

  Filled from CSS rather than with `currentColor`, because the two layers need two different
  alphas off one hue and both themes have to re-ink them.
#}
